"use client";
export function SearchField() {
  const id = `search-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 8)}`;
  return (
    <>
      <label htmlFor={id}>Search courses</label>
      <input id={id} type="search" />
    </>
  );
}

Browser console:
A tree hydrated but some attributes of the server rendered HTML didn''t match the client properties. This won''t be patched up.
  <label
+   htmlFor="search-k3f9qa"
-   htmlFor="search-0x7d2m"

Statement: "Replacing the random id with const id = useId() gives the same id on the server and in the browser, so this mismatch goes away and the label still points at its input."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Math.random() runs once on the server and again in the browser, so the two renders produce different ids, and React does not fix attributes after hydration ("This won''t be patched up"). useId derives the id from the component''s position in the tree, which is the same in both renders, so the label''s htmlFor and the input''s id match on both sides. The misconception is that any unique value will do; the value must be unique and also identical in both renders, which rules out random numbers, counters in module scope and timestamps.', 2, 5
