WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nrc_321'), 'A teammate fixes the action like this:

export async function changeCoursePrice(id: string, price: number) {
  await db.course.update({ where: { id }, data: { price } });
  revalidateTag(courseTag(id), "max");      // courseTag(id) === `course:${id}`
}

The instructor''s browser, Network tab, right after saving:
09:00:05  POST /instructor/courses/42      200  (server action)
09:00:07  GET  /course/nextjs-rendering    200  page shows 249000 VND
09:00:09  GET  /course/nextjs-rendering    200  page shows 199000 VND

Statement: "The old price at 09:00:07 is how revalidateTag with the \"max\" profile works, and calling updateTag(courseTag(id)) in this server action instead would show the new price on the first reload."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. In Next.js 16, revalidateTag(tag, "max") marks the tagged entries stale: the next request is served the old content while a fresh copy is built in the background, and the request after that gets the fresh copy (stale-while-revalidate). That is right for background events, but an instructor who just saved expects to see the change. updateTag, available only in Server Actions, expires the entries at once, so the next request waits for fresh data. The misconception is that every revalidation function removes the entry immediately; check which one you call, and in which version.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nrc_321') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
