WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nrc_331'), '$ curl -si https://api.codedev.test/course-page/42 -H "Cookie: session=LAN_SESSION"
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=300
Vary: Accept-Encoding
Content-Type: application/json

{"course":{"id":"42","title":"Next.js Rendering & Caching"},"enrollment":{"progress":35},"price":249000}

A CDN that honours Cache-Control sits in front of the API.

Statement: "Because the request carries Lan''s session cookie, the CDN keeps a separate copy for each learner, so Binh cannot receive Lan''s progress from it."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A shared cache keys a stored response by the URL and by the request headers named in Vary. Vary names only Accept-Encoding, and public, s-maxage=300 explicitly allows shared caches to store the response, so Binh''s request for the same URL within 300 seconds can be answered with Lan''s copy, progress included. The fix is to split the data: a public endpoint for the course with public caching, and a personal endpoint with Cache-Control: private, no-store. The misconception is that cookies automatically partition caches; they only do if the cache is configured to key on them, which also destroys the hit rate.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nrc_331') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
