    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nrc_421'), 'First attempt at fixing the course page:

export default async function CoursePage({ params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const user = await getSessionUser();
  const course = await getCourse(slug);                 // ~120 ms
  const progress = await getProgress(user.id, slug);    // ~1,400 ms
  return (
    <>
      <CourseHeader course={course} />
      <Suspense fallback={<ProgressSkeleton />}>
        <ProgressPanel progress={progress} />
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}

(ProgressPanel only renders the progress prop it receives; it awaits nothing.)

Statement: "Because ProgressPanel is wrapped in Suspense, the browser receives the course header after about 120 ms and the progress panel streams in later."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The page awaits getProgress itself before it returns any JSX, so the server has nothing to send until both loaders are done: the first byte still arrives after about 1.5 s. A Suspense boundary can only stream content that suspends below it; ProgressPanel receives finished data as a prop and never suspends, so the fallback is never even shown. The fix is to move the await into an async ProgressPanel under the boundary (pass slug, not progress). The common misconception is that wrapping a component in Suspense makes its data load later; what matters is where the await runs.', 1, 5
